{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Gerken2003-periodic-200layer-superprism",
  "Source": "M. Gerken and D. A. B. Miller, \"Multilayer thin-film structures with high spatial dispersion,\" Appl. Opt. 42, 1330-1345 (2003). doi:10.1364/AO.42.001330 - Sec. 2, text p. 1332 ('100-period dielectric stack consists of 150-nm alternating layers of SiO2 ... and Ta2O5 ... total stack thickness of 30 um on a quartz substrate'); Figs. 2, 4",
  "Notes": "Periodic SiO2/Ta2O5 stack used in reflection near the stop-band edge for superprism spatial beam shift (demultiplexing)\nSpecification: Large wavelength-dependent lateral shift of the beam passing twice through the stack, just outside the stop band (40 deg, p-pol)\nReported: Bloch theory: stop-band edge at 901 nm for 40 deg incidence (Fig. 4; 36.5 deg and 43.5 deg move it to ~893 and ~908 nm). Measured/calculated shift rises from ~0 at 866 nm to ~13 um at 892 nm (Fig. 2, read from plot).\nNotes: Light enters through the quartz substrate (n = 1.52) at 40 deg (angle in air, outside the tilted plate), p polarization; exit medium assumed air. Which material is next to the substrate is NOT stated - Ta2O5 first is assumed (as in the paper's Table 1 designs). Both layers 150 nm physical. The RP merit line is only a rough stand-in; the real spec is the spatial shift, which has no merit code.\nLayers from Sec. 2, text p. 1332 ('100-period dielectric stack consists of 150-nm alternating layers of SiO2 ... and Ta2O5 ... total stack thickness of 30 um on a quartz substrate'); Figs. 2, 4; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
